  "analysis": "Comparing candidate platforms against the selection criteria reveals three distinct architectural tradeoffs. DeepAgents (source_001) inherits LangGraph's graph state machine model (source_002), which excels at complex multi-step workflows with explicit state transitions and checkpointing. This makes it strong for orchestration-heavy enterprise use cases, but the graph abstraction adds cognitive overhead and can obscure control flow for simpler tasks. Pydantic AI (source_003) differentiates on typed tool definitions and structured outputs, prioritizing developer ergonomics and type safety. Its tradeoff is weaker native support for long-running, branching agent workflows compared to a graph-based runtime. The DeepSeek Harness (source_004) is explicitly reserved for the outer validation environment, meaning it is not a general-purpose platform candidate but a controlled evaluation wrapper; selecting it as a primary platform would conflate validation with production orchestration. A key architectural boundary emerges from source_006: static task decomposition belongs to the application layer, not the platform. Platforms that hard-code decomposition reduce flexibility, while those that leave it to the application layer (as DeepAgents does via LangGraph's state machine) remain more composable. The benchmark design (source_007) evaluates quality, cost, and critical-path latency, which favors platforms with fine-grained observability and low per-step overhead. Graph-based platforms may incur higher latency on critical paths due to state serialization, while typed-output platforms may reduce retry costs through schema validation. The routing research gap (source_005) — most LLM routing selects one model per request — implies that no candidate natively supports per-step model routing; platforms with graph state machines (DeepAgents) are better positioned to add this later because routing decisions can be modeled as state transitions. The scoring rubric (source_008) weights evidence accuracy and analysis depth, so the comparison must remain grounded in the cited claims rather than vendor marketing. Overall tradeoff: DeepAgents offers workflow expressiveness at the cost of complexity; Pydantic AI offers type safety and simplicity at the cost of orchestration depth; DSH is not a platform candidate but a validation boundary; and the benchmark's latency metric penalizes heavyweight orchestration unless critical paths are optimized.",
