  "analysis": "Comparing candidate platforms against selection criteria reveals a core architectural tradeoff between orchestration depth and routing specialization. DeepAgents (source_001) builds on LangGraph (source_002), inheriting explicit nodes, edges, durable state, and check-pointing. This makes it strong for complex multi-step workflows where state persistence and recovery matter, but it couples the platform to LangChain's ecosystem and may add orchestration overhead for simple routing tasks. The DeepSeek Harness (source_004) is explicitly reserved for the outer validation environment rather than the core routing loop, meaning it cannot serve as the primary routing engine; its role is limited to validating outputs, which constrains its selection as a standalone platform. The benchmark design (source_007) requires fixed task DAGs, multiple candidate models, per-node and per-task scoring, cost accounting, and critical-path latency. DeepAgents satisfies the DAG and state requirements via LangGraph, but per-node model selection is not native to LangGraph itself; it must be layered on top. The RefractRouter research gap (source_005) directly targets per-node model selection to improve the quality-cost-latency Pareto frontier, which aligns with the benchmark's scoring needs but is a research focus rather than a production platform. The task decomposition boundary (source_006) clarifies that static DAG construction belongs to the application layer, so any selected platform must accept a pre-built DAG and focus on routing decisions. Tradeoffs: DeepAgents offers mature orchestration and state management but requires custom routing logic and inherits LangChain dependency risk. A RefractRouter-style approach optimizes the exact quality-cost-latency tradeoff the benchmark measures but lacks production maturity and durable-state guarantees. DSH cannot be the core router by design, so it is only viable as a validation component. The scoring rubric (source_008) weights evi